session:pr811Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #772 was a follow-up from #744 (SERVICE federation executor wiring, epic #741): fetch_service_blocking deserialized each fetched SERVICE response against its own fresh, per-call scratch Datastore::new(64), since the async pre-resolution step in query.rs's handlers runs strictly before acquiring the real store's lock and therefore has no mutable access to it. This had two consequences for the rare triple-term/blank-node case: a GraphElement::TripleTerm binding carried GraphElementIds only valid against that one scratch store, and a blank-node binding allocated from a per-call store's own counter could numerically collide both with the real store's blank nodes and, more seriously, with a *different* SERVICE call's blank nodes in the same query -- the latter directly violates SPARQL 1.1 section 15.3's per-results-document blank-node-label scoping, since an unrelated 'b0' from two separate endpoints would be spuriously joined as equal by the natural join's derived PartialEq comparison." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:811 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr811Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr811 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr811Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Investigated the deferred-interning option first (resolving bindings lazily at join time against the real store, which already runs under the real store's lock) since it would have avoided the lock-ordering risk #744 deferred for, but the join step consumes already-fully-resolved GraphElement values, not raw unresolved labels, so threading a deferred-resolution representation through it would be a comparably large change to the restructuring #744 rejected -- not pursued for this issue's scope. Instead split fetch_service_blocking into a fetch step (returns the raw response body, no store access) and a new deserialize_fetched_bodies step run once every concurrent fetch for the query has been awaited, against one *shared* scratch Datastore (not one per call) seeded at a FEDERATED_BLANK_NODE_BASE offset (1 << 30) so its blank-node IDs also don't collide with the real store's own for any realistic dataset size. Sharing one store (not just seeding each call's own store from the same base) is what actually fixes the cross-call collision: from_sparql_json's own bnode-label-to-GraphElement map is already freshly created per call (so it was never the source of the bug), but store.new_anonymous_blank_node()'s counter only advances monotonically if the same store instance backs every call in the query. Verified via a dedicated regression test asserting the two calls' blank nodes are never joined as equal, plus added debug instrumentation during verification (since removed) to confirm the actual numeric IDs assigned diverge correctly across calls rather than trusting the test's pass/fail alone." ;
    agp:alternative "Triple-term bindings were deliberately left unfixed: a GraphElement::TripleTerm's subject/predicate/obj are GraphElementIds into whichever store resolves them later, and neither sharing the scratch store across calls nor offsetting its counter does anything for a binding whose correctness depends on IDs in a *different* store (the real query datastore) that this fix never touches. Filed as a follow-up, #809 (Status Todo), with a #[ignore]'d regression test (test_service_triple_term_binding_resolves_against_wrong_store) proving the gap rather than just asserting it in prose." .
